1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:03,600
Close your eyes.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:12,036
This isn't music. It's the Bay of Bengal: its
water and its rain, measured by NASA and played

3
00:00:12,036 --> 00:00:13,600
live by our code.

4
00:00:14,300 --> 00:00:17,700
We're Team PTSD, from Chattogram, Bangladesh.

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:23,700
Dipika Nath. Samonwita Sarker. Pratik Dev. Arnob
Mozumder. Rashed UL Islam.

6
00:00:24,300 --> 00:00:33,157
In Bangladesh, you hear the monsoon before you see
it. Rain on a tin roof. We know our weather by

7
00:00:33,157 --> 00:00:33,600
ear.

8
00:00:34,300 --> 00:00:40,312
NASA's Earth Information Center turns satellite
data into pictures: today's ocean, the last half

9
00:00:40,312 --> 00:00:41,600
hour of rain.

10
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:47,600
But a picture asks one thing of you first: that
you can see it.

11
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:55,700
Forty-three million people are blind. Nearly three
hundred million more live with serious sight loss.

12
00:00:56,300 --> 00:01:01,029
That's the problem behind Challenge fourteen:
Earth science shown through visualization alone

13
00:01:01,029 --> 00:01:03,000
limits who can reach it.

14
00:01:03,400 --> 00:01:09,306
The challenge: translate sight into sound, in real
time, from the Earth Information Center's own

15
00:01:09,306 --> 00:01:09,700
frames.

16
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:17,283
So we built Bloop for the people pictures leave
out. Not a soundtrack: the picture itself, as

17
00:01:17,283 --> 00:01:17,700
sound.

18
00:01:18,300 --> 00:01:25,200
Bloop reads each pixel of NASA's frame back into
its number, using the frame's own colour bar.

19
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:30,700
Warmer water, higher note. More rain, more drops.
West left, east right.

20
00:01:31,300 --> 00:01:38,784
This is how Bloop works with a screen reader.
Arrow keys move the needle; each step is spoken

21
00:01:38,784 --> 00:01:39,200
once.

22
00:01:40,900 --> 00:01:49,075
Enter gives the value and the place. P says
exactly where it came from. Over land, it says

23
00:01:49,075 --> 00:01:51,800
there's no data. It never guesses.

24
00:01:53,400 --> 00:02:01,600
Press B, and the same reading becomes braille, in
English or Bangla, with the needle as the cursor.

25
00:02:03,300 --> 00:02:10,657
For low vision: Alt and L. High contrast, larger
text, and a lens that follows the needle, the

26
00:02:10,657 --> 00:02:12,700
value in very large type.

27
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:18,700
Re-colour the map for your eyes, or get NASA's
colours back in one press.

28
00:02:19,300 --> 00:02:25,977
Our data is all NASA's: Earth Information Center
frames from the Scientific Visualization Studio,

29
00:02:25,977 --> 00:02:31,700
MUR sea surface temperature, GPM IMERG rain,
GISTEMP, and GRACE and GRACE-FO.

30
00:02:32,300 --> 00:02:38,714
And there's a whole rack behind it: twenty-three
official NASA records, a turntable Earth, Water's

31
00:02:38,714 --> 00:02:44,700
journey, and Ask Bloop in English or Bangla. All
of it from the keyboard.

32
00:02:45,300 --> 00:02:52,458
Every value is checked against NASA's own data.
Ocean: a median error of 0.31 degrees. Rain: about

33
00:02:52,458 --> 00:02:53,300
27 percent.

34
00:02:53,600 --> 00:02:57,200
We dropped NASA POWER: it disagreed with
independent records.

35
00:02:57,500 --> 00:03:03,700
And the music can't bend the data. Every note is
checked to within one cent.

36
00:03:04,300 --> 00:03:08,400
Dhaka, every April and May, from NASA GISTEMP. The
1980s…

37
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:13,600
…the last ten years.

38
00:03:18,300 --> 00:03:20,800
One point three seven degrees warmer.

39
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:25,800
And under the ground, GRACE shows Bangladesh's
stored water falling since 2002.

40
00:03:26,200 --> 00:03:32,200
Next, blind and low-vision listeners test it. They
decide what we build next.

41
00:03:32,800 --> 00:03:40,000
In 1997, the ocean made a sound no one could
explain: the Bloop. It was ice, breaking off

42
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:40,400
Antarctica.

43
00:03:40,700 --> 00:03:46,900
The Earth has always been telling us it's
changing. Now everyone can hear it.

